## MATRIX HOMESERVER ECONOMICS & CENTRALIZATION — measured evidence

Everything below was fetched this session (2026-09-04). Derived figures are marked DERIVED. Unreachable items marked NOT REACHED.

---

## 1. Matrix.org Foundation finances — the actual numbers

### The funding-crisis timeline (all direct quotes)

**2022-12-01** — https://matrix.org/blog/2022/12/01/funding-matrix-via-the-matrix-org-foundation/
> "even though there are over 5000 contributors to github.com/matrix-org — over 90% of the actual committed lines of code come from Element employees. Similarly, while we are enormously thankful for the past and existing generous donations from the wider Matrix community, **today they only come to $6,000 a month, relative to the $400,000 a month that Element has been funding**."

Membership tiers introduced here (still current, per https://matrix.org/foundation/members/): Individual $60/yr · Silver $2k–80k/yr · **Gold $200k/yr** · **Platinum $500k/yr**.

**2024-01-30** — https://matrix.org/blog/2024/01/2024-roadmap-and-fundraiser/ (Josh Simmons, then MD)
> "New members like Beeper and gematik — and hundreds of individuals — boosted our annual revenues **from £82K to £364K**. The open network has grown **from 80.3M to 115M addressable users**."
> "Element used to subsidize our activity, and **still donates upwards of £3M in core development annually** … While we managed a 400+% increase in revenue to £364K last year, **at £440K our expenses exceeded our revenue**. … the full cost of operating the Foundation at its current scope is projected to stabilize **somewhere north of £900K in 2024**."

Projected 2024 breakdown, verbatim:
- **£550K staffing** — 4 FTE (management, advocacy, standards, T&S) + external part-timers
- **£250K servers and other third-party providers**
- **£100K administrative** (legal, accounting, compliance)
- Comparison they drew: "the Python Software Foundation's expenses are nearer £2.9M, and **Signal's annual costs are in the ballpark of £39M**"
- Warning: "Otherwise, we need to start scaling back programs and staff **starting January 2025**."

**2024-12-25** — https://matrix.org/blog/2024/12/25/the-matrix-holiday-special-2024/
> "The Foundation has gone from being completely subsidized by Element in 2022, to having **nearly half of its annual budget covered by its 11 funding members**. … only being able to sustain half our annual budget is not nearly good enough, and it means that we **live hand-to-mouth, extending our financial runway a bit at a time**."

**2025-02-20 — "We're at a crossroads"** — https://matrix.org/blog/2025/02/crossroads/ (Thib, Robin Riley). The single densest financial disclosure:
> "the Foundation is not yet sustainable, despite running on the strictest possible budget, and is **burning through its (relatively small) reserves**. And we are at the point where **the end of the road is in sight**."
> "**unless the Foundation manages to raise $100,000 of funding by the end of March 2025**, we will … **shut down all the remaining bridges** hosted by the Matrix.org Foundation. This includes bridges to Slack, XMPP, OFTC (IRC), and Snoonet (IRC)."
> "the Foundation needs **an additional $610K in revenue to break-even** … this $100K would extend our runway **1 month**. … we nearly doubled our revenue in 2024, **reaching $561K**, but it was also the first year in which we carried the full cost of our operations: **$1.2M**. To make ends meet, we **liquidated $283K worth of cryptocurrency donations** and ended the year with a **$356K deficit**. We are currently on target for **$587K revenue in 2025**."

The bridges were in fact shut down in 2025 (annual report: "in 2025 the Foundation had to stop [bridging]").

**2025-06 — freemium/premium on matrix.org** — https://matrix.org/blog/2025/06/funding-homeserver-premium/
> "**20% of the Foundation's expenditure goes towards hosting the matrix.org free and public homeserver**. If we add in the cost of the moderation work done by the Trust and Safety team, the total share of the costs attributable to the matrix.org homeserver account for **almost 50% of all expenditure**. Meanwhile, today, **only 50% of the spending of the Foundation is covered by its revenues**."
> "before we get to the extreme position of **being forced to turn it off** leaving its **370k monthly active users** in the awkward position of finding a new home…"
> "The alternative is to turn off the server, which we want to avoid doing."

Free/premium limits now published at https://matrix.org/homeserver/pricing/ — Free: 10MB max attachment, 100MB/24h up to 500MB/28d. Premium: 100MB attachment, 1GB/24h up to 5GB/28d, invites "10 times faster". **Price still not published** ("Details of pricing have not yet been finalised") as of fetch date.

### The first Public Annual Report (FY2025 = Nov 2024–Oct 2025)

Blog: https://matrix.org/blog/2026/03/annual-report/ (2026-03-27, Amandine Le Pape)
PDF: https://matrix.org/foundation/reports/2025%20Public%20Annual%20Report.pdf (29 pp., fetched & extracted)

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Total costs FY2025 | **−£910,821** |
| Net loss FY2025 | **−£310,596** |
| Implied revenue FY2025 | **≈£600,225** (DERIVED) |
| Revenue growth | **+38%** |
| Loss as % of costs | **50% (FY2024) → 34% (FY2025)** |
| Staff fully funded | **4 FTE + 2 T&S contractors**; Managing Director post **vacant** |
| Funding members joined (cumulative) | **20 organisations** |

Revenue concentration, verbatim from the PDF:
> "We churned a Gold member, and the one left (**Automattic**) now corresponds to **50% of our revenue**."
> "The number of Silver members doubled, but only brought **30% more revenue** due to them mostly being small organisations in the Silver tier."
> "**We didn't get any grants.**"
> "Element's Platinum membership was supported by in-kind contributions and financial support in 2025, and thus **doesn't appear as revenue in financial reports**."
> "the Foundation is still **disproportionately dependent on Element's in-kind donations and financial support, which is unsustainable**."

Cost-reduction ceiling, verbatim:
> "the team is already at a minimal size, and the usage of the matrix.org server keeps increasing. We have been implementing quick wins to reduce the costs of matrix.org (like using Synapse Pro), but **moving it to cheaper infra or implementing bigger changes wouldn't have a groundbreaking impact and are projects we can't afford to start**."

Premium accounts: "**+400% since end of the 2024-2025 financial year**" in ARR (absolute figure is in a chart image, NOT REACHED as text).

### Current member roster (https://matrix.org/foundation/members/, fetched 2026-09-04)
- **Platinum (1):** Element Creations Ltd.
- **Gold (1):** Automattic
- **Silver (20):** Blender Foundation, connect2x, DINUM, Discourse, Ercom, Fairkom, Famedly, FlatTurtle, Gematik, IndieHosters, infra.run, Liberbyte, LiveKit, LU-CIX, meedio, Pangea Chat, Rocket.Chat, SSH, XWiki
- Plus 15 non-paying "Ecosystem" and 5 "Associate" members.

So: **two paying members supply the large majority of cash revenue**, one of which (Element) books as in-kind.

### Element's commercial shift
**2023-11-06** — https://element.io/blog/element-to-adopt-agplv3/ (updated 2023-12-13)
> "As a commercial entity, **Element has driven the bulk (more than 95%) of core Matrix development for the last seven years**, and maintains the largest Matrix homeserver (matrix.org) on behalf of the Matrix.org Foundation."
> "**Element is losing its ability to compete in the very ecosystem it has created.** It is hard for Element to innovate and adapt as quickly as companies whose business model is developing proprietary Matrix-based products and services without the responsibility and costs of maintaining the bulk of Matrix."

Synapse, Dendrite, sydent, sygnal relicensed Apache-2.0 → **AGPLv3**, repos moved matrix-org/ → element-hq/. Foundation's own response, 2024-08-15 (https://matrix.org/blog/2024/08/heart-of-matrix/): "these [questions] have become even more pressing for us **since Element switched developing Synapse and several other projects to AGPLv3, rather than contributing under the Foundation as Apache v2**."

Two-tier product split stated in the 2025 holiday post (https://matrix.org/blog/2025/12/24/matrix-holiday-special/): "**Synapse Pro** continues to add scalability and paid support for large deployments (alongside **ESS Pro**), following the philosophy that features which empower end-users end up in FOSS but **features which empower enterprises end up in Pro**."

*Element's specific 2023 "sustainability" post: **NOT REACHED** (element.io has no /blog/tag/sustainability; 404).*

