## 2. Centralization despite federation

### Foundation's own published counts

| Date | Figure | Source |
|---|---|---|
| end 2022 | **80.3M** addressable users | matrix.org 2024-01-30 |
| Jan 2024 | **115M** addressable users | matrix.org 2024-01-30 |
| Jan 2024 | matrix.org homeserver: "**over 250K daily active users**" | matrix.org 2024-01-30 |
| Jun 2025 | matrix.org homeserver: "**370k monthly active users**" | matrix.org 2025-06 |
| **31 Oct 2025** | **190 million known federated users on the open federation** | Annual Report p."Facts and Figures" |
| **31 Oct 2025** | **1.15 million monthly active users on the open federation** | ibid. |
| **31 Oct 2025** | **234 thousand daily active users on the Matrix.org homeserver** | ibid. |

DERIVED, and the load-bearing ratio: **matrix.org ≈ 32% of all monthly-active users in the entire open federation** (370k / 1.15M). Also note **matrix.org DAU fell from >250K (Jan 2024) to 234K (Oct 2025)** while the network's headline user count nearly doubled.

Caveat the Foundation states itself: "The number of users we can see in the open federation grew by almost 10 million in 2025. This doesn't include the users in closed networks, like the governments deployments typically are, **or those on servers who don't report home**." The instrument is **Synapse's opt-in phone-home stats** ("active users (based on Synapse opt-in phone-home reporting)", 2023 holiday update) — Synapse-only, opt-in, so 1.15M MAU is a *lower bound of reporting servers*, not the network. Also: 115M (Jan 2024) → 190M (Oct 2025) with "almost 10 million in 2025" implies a ~65M jump during 2024 that is not separately documented — treat the series as discontinuous.

### The Foundation admits the centralization problem in writing
**2024-03** — https://matrix.org/blog/2024/03/why-matrix-org/ ("A point of centralisation"):
> "why is the Matrix.org homeserver, **probably the largest homeserver in the whole federation**, still in open registration mode?"
> "Gmail.com provides nearly 50% of the active email accounts in 2023 … **The Matrix.org Foundation doesn't want to become the Gmail.com or Outlook.com of Matrix.** It doesn't generate any revenue from its users and runs on donations."
> "The technical maintenance costs **do not grow linearly** with the number of users."
> "Since agglomeration around a single instance is **against the goals of Matrix and its Foundation**, users need to have a way out and incentives to move."

The Annual Report notes "the community is looking to set-up a **'Matrix.org Homeserver Decentralisation Working Group'**".

### MEASURED crawl (the only peer-reviewed one)
**Jacob, Grashöfer, Hartenstein, "A Glimpse of the Matrix … Scalability Issues"** — arXiv:1910.06295 (v1 2019-10-14, v2 2019-11-29), also KIT 10.5445/ir/1000100364. Crawl snapshot date **2018-07-25** via the "DSN Traveller" bot, 798 public rooms:

- **2,003 homeservers**, **131,463 users** observed
- **largest server: 76,271 users** (= **58.0%** of all observed users, DERIVED); second: 37,751
- "**this upper 1% of homeservers comprises 87% of the 131,463 total users found**"
- "**Only 15 servers were seen having more than 100 users**"; most servers had ≤3 users
- Largest room: 24,729 users; largest-by-servers room spanned 581 servers = 76% of all known servers
- 83% of rooms had <10 servers; 71% of rooms had ≤100 users

And the structural finding that matters for economics — federation does **not** relieve load:
> "the single, **largest server is involved in 44.5% of all messages, sends 88.4%, but receives only 0.6%** of all messages."
> "**moving from a centralized network to a more decentralized one will not improve the load distribution, but can actually make it worse**: … the large server will have to distribute the events of one fewer user, but instead has to distribute all events from its remaining users to the new homeserver."
> "the network load for every server would then **grow linearly in the number of users participating in a room**, as the scalability effect of reaching multiple users with single message to a server vanishes."

**arXiv contains exactly one paper matching "Matrix federation" (total 1).** "Matrix homeserver" as a phrase: **0 results**. There is no published follow-up crawl.

