## 3. Synapse / homeserver resource cost — documented figures

**Official Synapse minimum** — https://github.com/element-hq/synapse/blob/develop/docs/setup/installation.md:
> "System requirements: POSIX-compliant system … Python 3.10 or later, up to Python 3.13. — **At least 1GB of free RAM if you want to join large public rooms like #matrix:matrix.org**"

**Official Dendrite sizing** — https://github.com/element-hq/dendrite/blob/main/docs/installation/1_planning.md:
> "At an absolute minimum, Dendrite will expect **1GB RAM**. For a comfortable day-to-day deployment which can participate in federated rooms for a number of local users, be prepared to assign **2-4 CPU cores and 8GB RAM — more if your user count increases**."
> "If you are running PostgreSQL on the same machine, **allow extra headroom for this too**."

**Official small-hardware guide** — https://github.com/element-hq/synapse/blob/develop/docs/other/running_synapse_on_single_board_computers.md:
> Platform tested: 2×1GHz armhf, **2GiB RAM**, SSD, Postgres. "It seems that once you get up to about **4x1.5GHz arm64 4GiB these issues are no longer a problem**."
> Presence: "Element web will frequently be saying the server is offline while the python process will be **pegged at 100% cpu**" — recommends `use_presence: false`.
> Joining: "Joining a 'large', federated room will initially fail … waiting a while (**10-60mins**) and trying again will succeed."
> Measured join times on that box: `29.9s` for #debian-fasttrack:poddery.com, `182.1s` for #decentralizedweb-general:matrix.org, **`911.6s` for #synapse:matrix.org**.
> Recommends `limit_remote_rooms: complexity: 3.0` (complexity = current_state_events / 500), i.e. **blocking any room over ~1,500 state events**.

**Database bloat — measured, real deployments:**
- https://github.com/matrix-org/synapse/issues/7472 (2020-05-12, MurzNN): `state_groups_state` **51 GB / 274,929,152 rows**, vs `event_json` 22 GB / 14.5M rows — **18× more rows than events**. Synapse maintainer richvdh's reply: "doesn't sound so bad to me."
- https://github.com/matrix-org/synapse/issues/3364 — "My HS has accumulated thousands of unreferenced state groups" — opened **2018-06-07, still OPEN, 76 comments**.
- https://github.com/matrix-org/synapse/issues/12821 (2022-05-20, OPEN) — state groups not purged when rooms are deleted.
- https://levans.fr/shrink-synapse-database.html (2020-06-11): personal homeserver "**shrunk my homeserver database from 100GB to a little under 8GB**"; compression got some rooms "down to **0.4% of the original size**"; a single REINDEX "**freed about 40GB**". I.e. an ordinary personal Synapse can carry ~92% waste.

**matrix.org itself, the ceiling case** — https://matrix.org/blog/2025/10/post-mortem/ (published 2025-10-29, incident 2025-09-02, **~24h outage**):
> "**51TB**" Postgres dataset; "when the database reached roughly **51TB (90% of disk capacity)** we set about adding more disks"
> "made of a main Synapse instance with **hundreds of workers**, backed by a single logical Postgres cluster made up of two machines … bare metal servers at **Mythic Beasts** … RAID 10 with mdraid"
> Restore from S3 + ~18h of WAL replay took **5.5 hours** ("we had been hoping … around 10 minutes for every 1 hour of WALs").

Also https://matrix.org/blog/2025/07/postgres-corruption-postmortem/ (index corruption "**at least a year**, likely significantly longer", undetected by `pg_amcheck`) — and the cost line: "it would leave the Matrix Foundation an EC2 bill of **hundreds of USD per day** for an EC2 instance large enough to host the database."

And https://matrix.org/blog/2024/04/morg-post-mortem/:
> "To perform an upgrade without downtime, the Foundation would need to add another server … **An extra server introduces a recurring cost and requires additional work that the Foundation cannot afford right now.**"

**Practitioner cost argument** — https://xn--gckvb8fzb.com/giving-up-on-element-and-matrixorg/ (2025-07-19, 115 pts / 146 comments on HN item 44617309): quotes the Dendrite 2-4 cores / 8GB figure and concludes "Matrix ends up being worse not just in terms of user experience, but also **from an administrative and cost perspective**, especially when compared to something like Ejabberd."
Related widely-read critiques: https://telegra.ph/why-not-matrix-08-07 (46 pts, HN 44714994) — architectural, not cost; https://blog.koehntopp.info/2024/02/13/the-matrix-trashfire.html (308 pts, HN 39368561) — **body NOT REACHED** (server returned a 336-byte stub to both curl and WebFetch).

